@lesson llm-pretraining
Q: Approximately how many FLOPs does it take to train a 7-billion-parameter dense model on 2 trillion tokens? Enter the value in units of \(10^{22}\).
= 8.4 ±0.2
> \(C\approx6ND=6\cdot7\times10^9\cdot2\times10^{12}=8.4\times10^{22}\).

Q: What did the Chinchilla analysis recommend for a fixed compute budget?
* Scale parameters and training tokens together, roughly 20 tokens per parameter
- Always use the largest possible model
- Train on as few tokens as possible
- Only scale data, never parameters
> Many models train far beyond that ratio because smaller over-trained models are cheaper to serve.

Q: Which stage first turns a pretrained base model into an instruction follower?
- Pretraining
* Supervised fine-tuning on instruction–response pairs
- Tokenizer training
- Quantization
> SFT comes after pretraining and before preference tuning or RL.

Q: Thanks to the causal mask, one forward pass over a sequence of T tokens trains on T next-token predictions.
= true
> Every position predicts its successor in parallel, which makes LM training efficient.

@lesson decoding
Q: What does a temperature below 1 do to the next-token distribution?
* Sharpens it toward the most likely tokens
- Flattens it toward uniform
- Removes the top token
- Nothing
> Dividing logits by \(T\lt1\) exaggerates their differences.

Q: How does top-p (nucleus) sampling choose candidate tokens?
- Always the top 50 tokens
* The smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability reaches p
- Tokens with probability above p
- A random subset of size p
> The candidate set adapts: small when the model is confident, large when it is not.

Q: Which decoding method is a good fit for translation but tends to be bland for open-ended chat?
- Top-p sampling at T = 1.2
* Beam search
- Random sampling
- Top-k with k = 1000
> Beam search finds high-likelihood sequences, which favours safe, repetitive text in open-ended generation.

@lesson finetuning
Q: About how many bytes per parameter does mixed-precision Adam training need (weights, gradients, master weights and two moments)?
= 16
> 2 + 2 + 4 + 8 bytes, before activations.

Q: How many trainable parameters does LoRA with rank 8 add to a single 4096×4096 matrix?
= 65536
> \(r(d+k)=8\cdot(4096+4096)=65{,}536\).

Q: Why is LoRA's \(\mathbf B\) matrix initialized to zero?
* So the model starts exactly as the pretrained model
- To save memory
- To make training random
- Because \(\mathbf A\) is also zero
> \(\mathbf B\mathbf A=0\) at the start, so the update begins as a no-op.

Q: Your assistant must answer questions about company policies that change monthly. What is the better first choice?
- Fine-tune monthly on the policies
* Retrieval-augmented generation over the current documents
- Increase the temperature
- Train a new model from scratch
> RAG handles changing knowledge and gives citations; fine-tuning suits style, format and behaviour.

@lesson alignment
Q: Why does RLHF add a KL penalty to the reference model?
* To stop the policy from drifting into odd outputs that exploit the reward model
- To speed up training
- To make outputs longer
- To remove the need for a reward model
> Reward hacking is the main risk when optimizing against a learned reward.

Q: What does DPO need that standard RLHF also needs?
- A separately trained reward model
- On-policy sampling during training
* Preference pairs (preferred and rejected responses)
- A value network
> DPO optimizes a classification-style loss directly on preference pairs and a reference model.

Q: In GRPO, how is the advantage of each sampled answer computed?
* By normalizing its reward against the mean and standard deviation of rewards in its group
- With a learned value function
- From human ratings only
- It is always 1
> Group-relative baselines remove the need for a critic.

@lesson inference
Q: A model has 32 layers, 8 KV heads of dimension 128, fp16. How many bytes of KV cache per token? (2 for K and V)
= 131072
> \(2\cdot32\cdot8\cdot128\cdot2=131{,}072\) bytes, about 128 KB per token.

Q: Why is generating output tokens memory-bound?
* Each new token requires reading all the weights and the KV cache with little computation per byte
- The GPU has too few cores
- Tokens are large
- The prompt is processed one token at a time
> Batching more sequences raises arithmetic intensity.

Q: Speculative decoding changes the output distribution of the large model.
= false
> The large model verifies the draft tokens, so the distribution is preserved while latency drops.

Q: What does a mixture-of-experts layer change?
- It uses every expert for every token
* Each token is routed to a few experts, so total parameters grow while per-token compute stays small
- It removes attention
- It reduces total parameters
> A load-balancing loss keeps the experts evenly used.

@lesson using-llms
Q: Which practices make LLM outputs more reliable in an application? (select all)
* Request JSON matching a schema and validate it in code
* Separate instructions from untrusted data with clear delimiters
* Maintain an evaluation set and rerun it on every change
- Trust outputs because the model sounds confident
> Validation, separation and regression testing are the basics of production prompting.

Q: LLM-as-judge evaluations have known biases. Which? (select all)
* Preference for longer answers
* Position bias between options
* Preference for outputs from the same model family
- They never disagree with humans
> Calibrate judges against human ratings on a sample.

Q: What is the most reliable way to reduce hallucinations about facts?
- Increase the temperature
* Ground answers in retrieved sources and check citations
- Ask the model to be honest
- Use a longer prompt
> Retrieved evidence plus verification beats instructions alone.

@lesson multimodal
Q: In a LLaVA-style model, what does the projector do?
* Maps vision-encoder outputs into the LLM's token embedding space
- Compresses the LLM
- Generates images
- Translates text
> Projected patch features become "visual tokens" that the language model reads.

Q: Which tasks are typical weaknesses of vision-language models? (select all)
* Counting objects
* Reading small text
* Fine spatial relations
- Describing the overall scene
> Evaluate on your own images, especially for these failure modes.

Q: In the first training stage of a LLaVA-style model, which part is usually trained?
- The whole LLM
* Only the projector, with the vision encoder and LLM frozen
- Only the vision encoder
- Nothing
> Alignment comes first; instruction tuning later unfreezes more.

@lesson reasoning
Q: What is self-consistency?
* Sampling several reasoning chains and taking the majority answer
- Asking the model to agree with itself
- Using temperature 0
- Fine-tuning on its own outputs
> Majority voting over diverse chains improves accuracy on problems with a single answer.

Q: What does a process reward model score?
- Only the final answer
* Individual reasoning steps
- The prompt
- Latency
> Step-level scores can guide search over reasoning paths.

Q: More test-time compute helps most on tasks with a reliable verifier, such as unit tests or checkable math answers.
= true
> Without a verifier, extra samples are hard to choose between.

@lesson llm-eval-safety
Q: From 20 samples with 3 correct, what is the unbiased pass@1 estimate?
= 0.15 ±0.001
> \(1-\binom{17}{1}/\binom{20}{1}=1-17/20=0.15\).

Q: What is benchmark contamination?
* Test items leaked into the model's training data, inflating scores
- A benchmark with typos
- Using too few examples
- Bias in the reward model
> Contaminated scores measure memorization rather than capability.

Q: What does a sparse autoencoder on model activations try to find?
- Smaller weights
* An overcomplete set of sparse, more interpretable features
- The training data
- The optimal learning rate
> Many learned features correspond to human-interpretable concepts.

Q: What is indirect prompt injection?
* Malicious instructions placed in content the model reads, such as a web page or document
- A typo in the system prompt
- A slow API call
- Using too many tokens
> Agents that read untrusted content and can act are the main target.

@lesson train-small-llm
Q: With a vocabulary of 16,000 tokens, what should the initial loss be roughly? (natural log, one decimal)
= 9.7 ±0.1
> A uniform prediction gives \(\ln16000\approx9.68\).

Q: Why shift the targets by one position when training a GPT?
* Each position must predict the next token
- To pad the sequence
- To skip the first word
- To make batches equal size
> Inputs are tokens 0..T−1 and targets are tokens 1..T.

Q: When instruction-tuning, the loss is usually masked so that it is computed only on…
- the system prompt
- the user's message
* the assistant's response tokens
- every token equally
> You want the model to learn to produce answers, not to imitate user prompts.

@lesson exam
Q: A 70B-parameter model is served in 4-bit precision. Roughly how many GB do the weights need?
= 35 ±3
> \(70\times10^9\times0.5\) bytes ≈ 35 GB, plus KV cache and overhead.

Q: Which changes reduce KV-cache memory per token? (select all)
* Grouped-query attention
* Quantizing the cache
- Increasing the number of KV heads
* Using fewer layers
> Memory is proportional to layers × KV heads × head dimension × bytes.

Q: A fine-tuned model writes in the right style but invents facts about new products. What is the fix?
* Add retrieval over product documentation and require citations
- Fine-tune longer
- Lower the learning rate
- Increase temperature
> Fine-tuning teaches style; facts that change belong in retrieval.

Q: Sorting these by when they occur in a typical LLM's life, which comes last?
- Pretraining
- Supervised fine-tuning
* Preference tuning (RLHF or DPO)
- Tokenizer training
> Tokenizer → pretraining → SFT → preference tuning or RL.

Q: Lowering temperature makes a model more knowledgeable.
= false
> Temperature changes randomness, not knowledge.
